WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.930
Speaker 1: Today, we're going to be speaking
with David Fidler, who's the writer, philosopher

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:08.750
and also author of the recently published
book Breakfast with Seneca, A Stoic Guide

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:14.380
to the Art of Living, which is a clear and
accurate guide to the teachings of the stoic

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:15.550
philosopher Seneca.

00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:16.810
David, it's really great having you on.

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:17.810
I appreciate it.

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:19.600
Speaker 3: Hey, it's great to speak with you,
David.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:26.480
Speaker 1: So particularly during the pandemic
on social media and elsewhere, stoicism as

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:32.750
a philosophy has has become very, very much
in vogue, with a lot of different people posting

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:35.310
everything from memes to articles about it.

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:42.830
And often there's this kind of very top level
understanding that stoicism means showing

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:48.300
little emotion and just sort of being only
slightly connected to the world around you,

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.000
which is not at all what the philosophy advocates
for.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.000
So.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.910
So maybe we could start there with what?

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:56.310
What is the what is stoicism, right?

00:00:56.310 --> 00:01:00.370
Speaker 3: That's a really good starting point
and stoicism.

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:04.220
It's an ancient Greek and Roman philosophy
of life.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:11.420
But the issue that you're alluding to is the
word stoic has actually changed to mean something

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:12.620
totally else.

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:17.250
Over the course of like many centuries, the
philosophy itself is like twenty three hundred

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:26.060
years old, and now stoicism means to repress
your emotions or keep a stiff upper lip.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:29.570
And that has nothing to do with the original
philosophy.

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:36.460
In fact, the Stoics were not unemotional in
any way.

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:40.250
They experienced regular emotions and they
thought they were fine.

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:46.610
The only problem that the Stoics had was with
extreme negative emotions, which they felt

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:48.820
arose from incorrect opinions.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:56.610
And they saw those extreme negative emotions
as being things like anger, worry, anxiety

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:57.610
and things like that.

00:01:57.610 --> 00:02:01.310
Speaker 1: So how did someone like Seneca?

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:07.120
What framework did Seneca advocate for for
dealing with those negative emotions?

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:10.070
Speaker 3: OK?

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:15.720
One of the most central teachings of stoicism,
this is something that all of the Roman Stoics

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:17.740
believed in.

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:22.870
And this is probably a huge takeaway for the
people who are listening to this discussion

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:29.459
today is that their idea was that we can't
control what actually happens to us, but we

00:02:29.459 --> 00:02:32.730
can control how we respond to things.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:34.830
They all believed in that.

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:41.920
And the main idea was that it's not really
things or events that upset us, but the opinions

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:44.670
or judgments that we hold about things.

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:50.750
And an example of this that I use in the book
is imagine it's like raining and you're walking

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:56.580
along one day and you walk by a big puddle
and a car comes by and splashes you and you're

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:57.840
covered with water.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:04.060
Well, someone might have the mental opinion
that, well, I just got splashed, which is

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:06.349
a pretty objective way of looking at things.

00:03:06.349 --> 00:03:11.520
But another person might think, Oh, you've
ruined my entire day and then develop a sense

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:17.230
of anger and rage about that and then want
to get even with the driver in some way.

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:24.020
So that shows how the opinions that we hold
about things really influence our emotional

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:31.629
states or even act as the seed of these extreme
negative emotions and this.

00:03:31.629 --> 00:03:32.629
It's not.

00:03:32.629 --> 00:03:35.930
Things are events that upset us, but the opinions
we hold about things.

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:41.900
That's the origin of what is now known as
cognitive therapy or cognitive behavioral

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:43.069
therapy.

00:03:43.069 --> 00:03:47.480
And the Stoics really invented cognitive therapy.

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:50.410
That's what their theory of emotion was about.

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:55.350
And the modern people, the modern psychologists
who invented cognitive therapy actually drew

00:03:55.350 --> 00:03:58.220
on these stoic ideas and mentioned the Stoics
by name.

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:02.440
Speaker 1: Yeah, you know, as I hear you talk
about it, there's this book from the 20th

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:08.459
century by Victor Frankl man's search for
meaning, which I don't think is overtly about

00:04:08.459 --> 00:04:09.739
stoicism.

00:04:09.739 --> 00:04:17.109
But he lays out in the context of surviving
the Holocaust a very similar idea about he

00:04:17.109 --> 00:04:23.370
could not control his circumstances, but he
could control the reaction he had to those

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:25.460
circumstances, which seems quite similar.

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:27.599
Speaker 3: Exactly.

00:04:27.599 --> 00:04:35.319
And I think there's a very famous saying attributed
to him that our freedom exists and the fact

00:04:35.319 --> 00:04:41.439
that we're able to pause before we make a
decision or a judgment.

00:04:41.439 --> 00:04:43.969
And that's really our source of freedom now.

00:04:43.969 --> 00:04:49.889
Apparently, he didn't say that he went back
to Rome, but the thought is the same and certainly

00:04:49.889 --> 00:04:54.990
his his idea of, you know, is an exact parallel
to that of the Stoics.

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:58.740
Speaker 1: One of the kind of big pushbacks
and reactions.

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:06.180
As I see to this recent popularization of
stoicism, our comments along the lines of,

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:11.330
yeah, that's really great to think about if
your basic needs are met and you have time

00:05:11.330 --> 00:05:14.449
to hang out on social media or reading books.

00:05:14.449 --> 00:05:18.659
But if you're not sure where your next meal
is coming from or you don't have housing or

00:05:18.659 --> 00:05:21.669
you need health care and can't afford it.

00:05:21.669 --> 00:05:26.259
Stoicism is not useful in that way, and it's
another one of these sort of now becoming

00:05:26.259 --> 00:05:30.189
commercialized ideas that spread on social
media.

00:05:30.189 --> 00:05:35.240
Well, how would you react to that critique
of the popularization of stoicism?

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:42.389
Speaker 3: Well, I don't think that's really
true, because now Seneca actually was very

00:05:42.389 --> 00:05:43.389
wealthy.

00:05:43.389 --> 00:05:47.150
The person the story philosopher I wrote the
book about, he was actually one of the wealthiest

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:49.000
people in the Roman Empire.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:54.589
But the earlier Greek Stoics weren't wealthy
at all, and the most famous story to follow

00:05:54.589 --> 00:05:57.259
him in Rome was Epictetus.

00:05:57.259 --> 00:06:03.120
Epictetus was maybe like about 15 when Seneca
died, and he was actually a slave.

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:12.240
So it was a philosophy that was embraced from
slaves to Roman emperors, and certainly all

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:16.599
of these stoic ideas are applicable.

00:06:16.599 --> 00:06:20.249
You know, by anyone today, it doesn't matter
on your level of wealth.

00:06:20.249 --> 00:06:27.159
In fact, the Stoics believed that our true
goods lie within us, and they believe that,

00:06:27.159 --> 00:06:33.399
you know, it's very valuable and it's an advantage
to have like wealth, a beautiful family, a

00:06:33.399 --> 00:06:34.809
beautiful house.

00:06:34.809 --> 00:06:41.710
But it's only our inner character that allows
us to use those things in a good way.

00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:47.280
So for the Stoics, the most important part
of life was really developing a good character.

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:50.629
And that's something they would say holds
true for everyone with.

00:06:50.629 --> 00:07:03.189
Speaker 1: If I recall correctly, Seneca had
some views on crowds and mob mentality that

00:07:03.189 --> 00:07:07.779
seemed like they might be relevant today to
some degree, including to to media and sort

00:07:07.779 --> 00:07:11.710
of some of the other modern things that humans
today are presented with.

00:07:11.710 --> 00:07:13.400
Can you talk a little bit about that?

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:15.590
Speaker 3: Sure.

00:07:15.590 --> 00:07:20.500
Actually, Seneca has this story about how
he went to see the gladiatorial games and

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:26.219
then people were being killed in front of
his eyes and the crowd was cheering for the

00:07:26.219 --> 00:07:29.889
death of the gladiators.

00:07:29.889 --> 00:07:32.969
And the other people that were thrown into
the ring.

00:07:32.969 --> 00:07:38.539
And he was actually, as far as I can tell
the first person to write about what is now

00:07:38.539 --> 00:07:46.979
called a crowd or mob psychology, which was
a study that developed in like the mid-1800s.

00:07:46.979 --> 00:07:53.530
And people now study things like emotional
contagions, things like that, and also how

00:07:53.530 --> 00:08:00.449
we unconsciously assimilate the behavior of
people that were exposed to.

00:08:00.449 --> 00:08:08.219
And this this holds very true in today's sort
of like internet world or Twitter world where

00:08:08.219 --> 00:08:15.889
you know, you get these emotional contagions
that seem to that seemed to float through

00:08:15.889 --> 00:08:16.889
the internet.

00:08:16.889 --> 00:08:21.469
And they're very contagious because sometimes
you'll see like a video clip and you don't

00:08:21.469 --> 00:08:23.379
have the full context of it.

00:08:23.379 --> 00:08:27.149
And people are making judgments about it and
denouncing it and things like that.

00:08:27.149 --> 00:08:31.209
And it's very easy to get swept up in that
kind of behavior.

00:08:31.209 --> 00:08:33.140
And Seneca warned about that specifically.

00:08:33.140 --> 00:08:40.919
If he was alive today, he would say, you know,
take a step back and don't assent, you know,

00:08:40.919 --> 00:08:47.240
immediately to the judgment of the crowd or
the mob, but wait until things become clarified

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:51.490
and also, you know, be mindful of due process
as well.

00:08:51.490 --> 00:08:54.940
You can't make those judgments just based
on a tweet.

00:08:54.940 --> 00:08:57.880
Oftentimes, usually, you need further information.

00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:07.920
Speaker 1: What was you write in the book
about the value of time and of not postponing

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:10.900
what one wants to do sort of indefinitely?

00:09:10.900 --> 00:09:15.740
Or, I mean, maybe even to any degree beyond
what's kind of logistically necessary?

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:18.960
Can you talk a little bit about that and the
connection to stoicism?

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:20.520
Speaker 3: Right?

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:23.350
Well, this is something that's really specific
to Seneca.

00:09:23.350 --> 00:09:27.700
He writes about this a lot, and he says that
time is our most valuable asset.

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:32.600
So people are very careful about the way that
they guard their wealth.

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:37.700
But when it comes to guarding their time,
they don't even think about it usually.

00:09:37.700 --> 00:09:44.860
And his idea is that people spend a lot of
time rushing around and acting as though they're

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:49.660
preoccupied with things, whereas it's more
of an act sometimes.

00:09:49.660 --> 00:09:56.070
And he felt that we should be very careful
about the way that we use our time.

00:09:56.070 --> 00:09:59.160
What I would say he actually talks about,
for example.

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:07.020
Like how people believe that they'll someday
retire and they spend their entire life working,

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:10.960
but then he says, how do you know that you'll
live to be 50 or 60 years old?

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:12.940
You could die any moment.

00:10:12.940 --> 00:10:18.260
And his his idea was like finding some kind
of balance between work and leisure.

00:10:18.260 --> 00:10:24.720
And he would have agreed with the modern idea
that we should work to live rather than live

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:25.720
to work.

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:33.020
Speaker 1: Yeah, that, you know, sometimes
you you get these distortions, I guess I would

00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:39.040
say of some of these precepts and they are
sort of exaggerated and taken to the extreme

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:42.940
where like what you just said would be taken
to mean, you know, this kind of you only live

00:10:42.940 --> 00:10:46.510
once, don't plan for the future at all kind
of mentality.

00:10:46.510 --> 00:10:51.330
But really, it seems to me the value in thinking
about these things is about finding some kind

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:56.010
of balance that that makes sense rather than
picking some extreme to adhere to.

00:10:56.010 --> 00:10:57.090
Speaker 3: Exactly.

00:10:57.090 --> 00:11:01.500
And that's something that everyone really
has to deal with on their own because everyone's

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:02.500
life is different.

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:07.610
So how one person will find the kind of balance
between life and work will be different than

00:11:07.610 --> 00:11:08.610
another person.

00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:11.400
But it's certainly an important question to
think about

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:16.861
Speaker 1: going back a little bit to the
social contagion thing, which you know you

00:11:16.861 --> 00:11:20.470
explained and I think is really clear when
it comes to like stuff on the internet as

00:11:20.470 --> 00:11:21.610
an example.

00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:27.441
When you look at the socio political system,
maybe specifically in the United States, what

00:11:27.441 --> 00:11:31.940
are some other areas in which this social
contagion is taking place that would be interesting

00:11:31.940 --> 00:11:33.270
to to be aware of?

00:11:33.270 --> 00:11:39.360
Speaker 3: Well, I think actually one of the
reasons that this has become such a phenomenon

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:43.090
is it's exacerbated by the internet.

00:11:43.090 --> 00:11:49.410
And another aspect of it also is that the
internet has been very good for many things.

00:11:49.410 --> 00:11:52.970
For example, we can have discussions like
this, but in other ways, I think it's been

00:11:52.970 --> 00:11:59.670
very, very negative because people base journalistic
coverage now on the number of clicks that

00:11:59.670 --> 00:12:00.670
stories get.

00:12:00.670 --> 00:12:04.410
So journalism has become very clickbait.

00:12:04.410 --> 00:12:09.150
And it's also really harmed, you know, serious
print journalism.

00:12:09.150 --> 00:12:19.170
So basically, journalists and the media have
every reason to encourage people getting angry

00:12:19.170 --> 00:12:24.060
about things, for example, because you know,
it's those stories that make people angry

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:28.630
that usually have the highest, you know, click
through rates.

00:12:28.630 --> 00:12:29.630
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:12:29.630 --> 00:12:36.590
And I mean, we see that daily where a story
that we spend a while researching about to

00:12:36.590 --> 00:12:41.870
take today, you know, the build up to what's
going on with Russia and Ukraine, right?

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:48.400
It just is not going to generate even a fraction
of the interest that Joe Biden calling that

00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:52.800
Fox reporter a son of a bitch yesterday is
going to generate.

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:58.180
And if you ask me, which is most substantively
valuable, it's obviously the geopolitical

00:12:58.180 --> 00:13:02.970
impact with historical implications of the
Ukraine Russia thing.

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:07.500
But it's not it for all the reasons you're
talking about, it's just not going to do as

00:13:07.500 --> 00:13:08.500
well.

00:13:08.500 --> 00:13:10.060
Speaker 3: Right?

00:13:10.060 --> 00:13:14.850
You know, another thing, too, is that you
have this huge media platforms now.

00:13:14.850 --> 00:13:18.710
And so they have a certain amount of space
allotted for, you know, the so-called news

00:13:18.710 --> 00:13:22.100
so that if nothing's happening, they actually
have to fill it with something.

00:13:22.100 --> 00:13:23.100
Yes.

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:24.100
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:13:24.100 --> 00:13:29.200
No, that that's that's certainly an issue
as well when it comes to Marcus Aurelius is

00:13:29.200 --> 00:13:33.390
often sort of your book focuses on Seneca,
but you mentioned Seneca, Epictetus, Marcus

00:13:33.390 --> 00:13:39.390
Aurelius kind of the third in the maybe most
three known Stoics.

00:13:39.390 --> 00:13:45.090
What does his perspective did his perspective
differ in significant ways from that of Seneca?

00:13:45.090 --> 00:13:48.090
Speaker 3: Mm hmm.

00:13:48.090 --> 00:13:51.990
It's really interesting because when I was
researching the book, I looked into this in

00:13:51.990 --> 00:14:00.620
a great deal of depth, and it's surprising
how similar the views of the three main Roman

00:14:00.620 --> 00:14:02.420
Stoics were.

00:14:02.420 --> 00:14:08.100
For example, an average reader wouldn't notice
this, but.

00:14:08.100 --> 00:14:13.270
All three of the Roman Stoics believed that
when you were on your deathbed that you should

00:14:13.270 --> 00:14:18.450
feel a sense of gratitude when you're dying
for the gift of life that the universe has

00:14:18.450 --> 00:14:19.690
given to you.

00:14:19.690 --> 00:14:20.690
Hmm.

00:14:20.690 --> 00:14:25.600
And I find that to be amazing, and that for
them would be a mark of a life that was, well,

00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:26.600
well lived.

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:30.400
And that was the whole point of philosophy
is to live a life that's truly worth living.

00:14:30.400 --> 00:14:36.410
So if you felt a deep sense of regret on your
deathbed, that would probably mean that you

00:14:36.410 --> 00:14:40.630
felt that you had made some very serious errors
in your life.

00:14:40.630 --> 00:14:45.930
Another idea that I think is very important
for people today that comes from the Stoics,

00:14:45.930 --> 00:14:47.870
all of the Stoics believed in this.

00:14:47.870 --> 00:14:51.779
They were the most pro-social philosophers
in the ancient world, and they taught that

00:14:51.779 --> 00:14:54.010
we should all contribute to society.

00:14:54.010 --> 00:14:55.770
Marcus Aurelius was obsessed with this.

00:14:55.770 --> 00:15:01.930
His meditations is a pretty short book, but
he he reminds himself like, this is personal

00:15:01.930 --> 00:15:02.930
notebook.

00:15:02.930 --> 00:15:07.050
He reminds himself 80 times to contribute
to the common good.

00:15:07.050 --> 00:15:13.370
And and the Stoics didn't believe these things
in the way that a religious person would believe

00:15:13.370 --> 00:15:14.690
them because they were philosophers.

00:15:14.690 --> 00:15:16.460
So they always had an argument for this.

00:15:16.460 --> 00:15:21.440
And their argument was that all people are
born with the faculty of Reason, which makes

00:15:21.440 --> 00:15:23.880
us rational creatures.

00:15:23.880 --> 00:15:26.310
And it also makes us equal to one another.

00:15:26.310 --> 00:15:32.320
So the Stoics were the first philosophical
school to say that human beings were equal

00:15:32.320 --> 00:15:34.940
to one another, the idea of human equality.

00:15:34.940 --> 00:15:40.480
And it also means that because we have this
spark of reason in common, there's a, you

00:15:40.480 --> 00:15:46.220
know, universal brotherhood or sisterhood
of humanity that we're all part of.

00:15:46.220 --> 00:15:49.170
And that means we should help each other.

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:52.500
And over the course of centuries, it took
a very long time.

00:15:52.500 --> 00:15:58.530
This stoic idea led to the idea of human rights
equality and ultimately to the elimination

00:15:58.530 --> 00:15:59.530
of slavery.

00:15:59.530 --> 00:16:05.310
In some ways, because the Enlightenment philosophers
who are talking about human rights and criticizing

00:16:05.310 --> 00:16:10.170
the institution of slavery were very familiar
with these stoic views and drew upon them

00:16:10.170 --> 00:16:14.000
Speaker 1: incredible historical context.

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:19.140
And some of what we've been talking about
today is in the book Breakfast with Seneca,

00:16:19.140 --> 00:16:21.890
A Stoic Guide to the Art of Living.

00:16:21.890 --> 00:16:27.120
We've been speaking with the book's author,
writer and philosopher David Fidler.

00:16:27.120 --> 00:16:28.830
Really appreciate your time today and your
insights.

00:16:28.830 --> 00:16:29.830
Speaker 3: Great.

00:16:29.830 --> 00:16:31.390
It's been wonderful speaking with you.

